SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 313 | Tuesday, March 17, 2026

Good morning.

1) Seven carriers file the full $300 April GRI; three file $150
The split tells you who has ships to fill. The three filing the smaller increase are the ones that added the most capacity to the transpacific last year. Shippers with contracts signed in the last two weeks are insulated; everyone else should assume $150 sticks for a month and fades.

2) The two largest container lessors agree to merge
Combined fleet of about 12 million TEU, roughly a third of the world's leased boxes. Regulators in three jurisdictions will look at it. Carriers are unhappy, which is the point; lessors have had no pricing power since 2022 and a merger is how you get some.

3) Baltimore's shipping channel returns to full depth
Two years after the bridge collapse, the Coast Guard confirmed the 50-foot channel is fully restored and the temporary restrictions are lifted. The port lost about 15% of its container volume to Norfolk and New York in the interim and has recovered perhaps half of it. Autos and farm equipment never left.

NUMBER OF THE DAY: 4.6%. The 10-year Treasury yield this morning, which is why every warehouse developer is telling you the deal "doesn't pencil."

That's the morning. Reply with tips.

